{
  "tema": 10848,
  "slug": "me-te-nos-directo",
  "titulo": "Direct object pronouns me, te, nos",
  "subtitulo": "¿Me ves? Sí, te veo.",
  "foto": "t02.jpg",
  "regla": "Me, te and nos are the direct object pronouns for me, you (tú) and us. They go right before the conjugated verb: Te veo mañana. Os is the vosotros form; in Mexico, for ustedes use los / las.",
  "comparada_kwiziq": null,
  "secciones": [
    {
      "id": "regla",
      "titulo": "The rule",
      "bloques": [
        {
          "tipo": "texto",
          "texto": "A **direct object** receives the action directly: _she sees **me**_, _I call **you**_, _they invite **us**_."
        },
        {
          "tipo": "tabla",
          "encabezados": [
            "Person",
            "Pronoun",
            "Example"
          ],
          "filas": [
            [
              "yo",
              "**me**",
              "Ana **me** invita. _Ana invites me._"
            ],
            [
              "tú",
              "**te**",
              "Yo **te** llamo. _I'll call you._"
            ],
            [
              "nosotros, nosotras",
              "**nos**",
              "El profe **nos** espera. _The teacher is waiting for us._"
            ],
            [
              "vosotros, vosotras",
              "**os**",
              "Not used in Mexico."
            ],
            [
              "ustedes",
              "**los, las**",
              "**Los** veo mañana. _I'll see you (all) tomorrow._"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "tipo": "texto",
          "texto": "The pronoun goes **before the conjugated verb**. With **no**, the order is **no + pronoun + verb**: **No te** oigo."
        },
        {
          "tipo": "texto",
          "texto": "With an infinitive you can also attach it to the end: **Te** voy a llamar = Voy a llamar**te**."
        },
        {
          "tipo": "texto",
          "texto": "**Me, te, nos** look exactly like the indirect and reflexive pronouns. Only the job changes."
        },
        {
          "tipo": "texto",
          "texto": "Tip: think of the pronoun as a sticker you slap on the front of the verb: **me** ves, **te** veo, **nos** ven."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ejemplos",
      "titulo": "Examples",
      "bloques": [
        {
          "tipo": "ejemplos",
          "lista": [
            {
              "es": "¿==Me== escuchas? Se corta la llamada.",
              "en": "Can you hear me? The call keeps cutting out."
            },
            {
              "es": "==Te== quiero mucho, abuela.",
              "en": "I love you so much, Grandma."
            },
            {
              "es": "Mi entrenador ==nos== espera en el gimnasio a las siete.",
              "en": "My coach is waiting for us at the gym at seven."
            },
            {
              "es": "¿==Me== llevas a la playa mañana?",
              "en": "Will you take me to the beach tomorrow?"
            },
            {
              "es": "No ==te== conozco. ¿Cómo te llamas?",
              "en": "I don't know you. What's your name?"
            },
            {
              "es": "==Nos== invitaron a una carne asada en Playas.",
              "en": "They invited us to a carne asada in Playas."
            },
            {
              "es": "Mañana ==te== llamo, ¿va?",
              "en": "I'll call you tomorrow, OK?"
            },
            {
              "es": "Voy a esperar==te== afuera.",
              "en": "I'm going to wait for you outside."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
